# UC08 — DDQ / Questionnaire Response

**Persona**: Sales engineer, compliance lead, IR ops, or RFP coordinator responding to a heavy customer/investor/auditor questionnaire.

**Scenario**: A buyer, auditor, investor, or regulator sends a 100-question structured questionnaire — operational due diligence (ODD), SIG / SIG-Lite, CAIQ, vendor security questionnaire, RFP, or LP investor DDQ. Every question must be answered from the responding organization's own documentation pack: policies, SOC2 report, ISO certificates, prior questionnaire responses, product documentation, security architecture docs.

This is a flagship Normain use case — high-volume, structured, citation-required, often compliance-graded. Real Normain customers run these monthly.

**Mode**: **Simple**. The 100 questions are distinct hand-written items — not a template × varying input column. Each question stands alone. The "100 questions on one corpus" pattern is what makes it a separate Simple-mode test from UC01/UC06/UC07.

**Question types**:
- Structured answer generation (often Yes/No/N/A + free-text justification)
- Citation from own corpus (where in our policies/SOC2 is this answered?)
- Classification (in-scope / out-of-scope / partial)
- Consistency across related questions (does our answer to Q23 contradict Q47?)
- Mapping between control frameworks (SIG question → SOC2 control reference)

**Sample docs (~25)**: Normain's own publicly-available materials as a faithful proxy — `ops_security.docx`, `secure_dev_policy.docx`, SOC2 Type II report, ISO 27001 cert + Statement of Applicability, sub-processor list, BCP/DR docs, product security whitepaper, prior-year SIG response (if available).

**Question count**: 100 — this is the use case where the question volume genuinely is the test. SIG-Lite has ~125 questions; CAIQ has ~261. 100 is a realistic mid-size DDQ.

**What this tests**: Question-volume ceiling on a single corpus. Structured response generation with consistent voice + accurate self-citation. Many competitors degrade past 30-40 questions in a single session — context bleed, repetition drift, contradictory answers across the same questionnaire. Normain's repeatability story plays hardest here.
